---
import "../styles/global.css";
import BallotPaper from "../components/BallotPaper.astro";
import CandidateStack from "../components/CandidateStack.astro";
import HeroIllustration from "../components/HeroIllustration.astro";
import { scenarioExplore } from "../data/scenario-explore";
import { scenarioSpoiler } from "../data/scenario-spoiler";
import { strategicBallot } from "../data/sample-ballot-strategic";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-AU">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Who Really Won? — FPTP vs. preferential voting</title>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fraunces:opsz,wght@9..144,500;9..144,600&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500;600&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      <nav aria-label="Primary">
        <a href="./">Who Really Won?</a>
      </nav>
    </header>
    <main>
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="intro-heading">
        <div class="hero-content">
          <h1>Who Really Won?</h1>
          <h2 id="intro-heading">Same votes, different winner</h2>
          <p>
            You've probably marked a ballot paper before — a slip of paper
            where you tick who you want to win. What's easy to miss is that
            the rules for turning a stack of marked ballots into a winner
            aren't fixed. Keep every voter's choice exactly the same, change
            only how those ballots get counted, and a different candidate can
            come out on top. This page walks through one hypothetical
            election, counted two different ways, so you can watch that
            happen for yourself.
          </p>
        </div>
        <HeroIllustration />
        <a
          class="hero-scroll-cue"
          href="#fptp-ballot-intro"
          aria-label="Scroll to how the ballot works"
        >
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M4 8 L12 16 L20 8" />
          </svg>
        </a>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="fptp-ballot-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Every voter gets one ballot paper. Under first-past-the-post, you
            tick just one box — your favourite candidate — and that's the
            only preference this system ever sees.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz chapter-viz-intro" id="fptp-ballot-intro">
          <h2 id="fptp-ballot-heading">How the ballot works</h2>
          <BallotPaper
            candidates={scenarioExplore.candidates}
            ranking={scenarioExplore.groups[0].ranking}
            variant="full"
            mode="fptp"
            hero
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="explore-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Every voter ranks the candidates in order, but under
            first-past-the-post only a voter's first preference is ever
            counted. Try it yourself — drag a candidate's stack and watch
            who's ahead.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="explore-app">
          <h2 id="explore-heading">How first-past-the-post works</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioExplore.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="spoiler-chapter" aria-labelledby="spoiler-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Now here's one particular election, and it shows a real flaw
            called the spoiler effect: when two similar candidates split a
            majority that would rather see either of them win, a
            less-preferred third candidate can win instead. Aster and Birch
            are that pair here — split their support, and first-past-the-post
            hands the win to Cedar on a minority of first preferences. Try
            dragging the stacks: as long as Aster and Birch are close
            together, Cedar keeps winning even though most voters prefer
            someone else.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="spoiler-app">
          <h2 id="spoiler-heading">The spoiler effect: where first-past-the-post goes wrong</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioSpoiler.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="strategic-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Under first-past-the-post, a vote for a candidate who can't win
            is a vote that changes nothing. Take one Birch supporter from the
            scenario above: Birch is the weaker of the two similar
            candidates, so backing them sincerely leaves Cedar's plurality
            untouched. Backing Aster instead — their genuine second choice —
            actually changes the outcome.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz">
          <h2 id="strategic-heading">Why voters vote tactically</h2>
          <div class="ballot-comparison">
            <div>
              <h3>Sincere vote</h3>
              <BallotPaper
                candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
                ranking={strategicBallot.sincereRanking}
                variant="full"
                mode="fptp"
              />
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Tactical vote</h3>
              <BallotPaper
                candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
                ranking={strategicBallot.tacticalRanking}
                variant="full"
                mode="fptp"
              />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="duverger-heading">
        <h2 id="duverger-heading">Why elections converge to two parties</h2>
        <p>
          This is the pattern behind Duverger's law: when voters know that
          backing their sincere favourite can hand the win to their least
          preferred candidate, they drift toward whichever of the two
          strongest contenders they dislike least. Over enough elections,
          first-past-the-post tends to squeeze out third parties and settle
          into a two-party contest — not because voters stop having a wider
          range of preferences, but because the system only ever listens to
          the first one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="irv-ballot-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            We just watched first-past-the-post hand the win to Cedar even
            though most voters preferred someone else. Here's the fix:
            preferential voting — also called instant-runoff voting, or IRV
            — asks more of every ballot in the first place. Instead of
            ticking one box, number every candidate in the order you
            actually prefer them: 1 for your favourite, 2 for your next, and
            so on, all the way down.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz chapter-viz-intro" id="irv-ballot-intro">
          <h2 id="irv-ballot-heading">How preferential ballots work</h2>
          <BallotPaper
            candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
            ranking={scenarioSpoiler.groups[1].ranking}
            variant="full"
            mode="irv"
            hero
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="recount-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Here's that same election again, but counted a different way.
            Instant-runoff voting uses every voter's full ranking: if no one
            has a majority of first preferences, the last-place candidate is
            eliminated and their ballots move to each voter's next choice.
            Step through the recount and watch who wins this time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="recount-app">
          <h2 id="recount-heading">Recounting the same election under IRV</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioSpoiler.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <div class="recount-status">
            <p data-testid="round-status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
          </div>
          <div class="recount-controls">
            <button type="button" data-action="prev-round">Previous round</button>
            <button type="button" data-action="next-round">Next round</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="conclusion-heading">
        <h2 id="conclusion-heading">So, who really won?</h2>
        <p>
          Same voters, same ballots, same election — but two different
          winners, depending only on the counting rule. Under
          first-past-the-post, Cedar won because Aster and Birch split a
          majority that would rather have seen either of them win — the
          spoiler effect from earlier. Under preferential voting, that
          split gets resolved instead of ignored: once Birch is eliminated,
          their voters' next preference flows to Aster, and the candidate
          more voters actually preferred wins instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          That also eases the deeper problem from Duverger's law: voters
          drift toward whichever of two strong contenders they dislike
          least because a sincere vote for anyone else risks a result like
          Cedar's — exactly the bind Aster and Birch's supporters were in.
          Preferential voting removes that bind. Ranking a smaller or newer
          party first costs nothing, because an eliminated candidate's
          ballots simply flow to the next preference instead of vanishing.
          It won't rebuild a multi-party system on its own, but it removes
          the specific incentive first-past-the-post creates to vote for
          someone you don't actually want.
        </p>
        <p>
          The votes never changed. The rules did — and that was enough to
          change who won.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="freeplay-heading">
        <h2 id="freeplay-heading">Try it yourself, no script this time</h2>
        <p>
          Every example above was tuned to make a point. This one isn't — add
          or remove candidates, drag any stack however you like, and see who
          first-past-the-post picks.
        </p>
        <div id="freeplay-app">
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-freeplay-columns></div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <div class="freeplay-controls">
            <button type="button" data-action="add-candidate">
              Add candidate
            </button>
            <button type="button" data-action="switch-system">
              Switch to preferential voting
            </button>
          </div>
          <div class="freeplay-recount" data-freeplay-recount hidden></div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <script>
      import "../scripts/bootstrap";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
